# What is Wyrd

<Aside title="In one sentence" data-variant="wyrd">

Wyrd gives every part of an AI system — a dataset, a model, a prompt, an agent —
one typed, versioned record called a **Card**, stores those cards in a
**registry**, and runs agents and workflows on the **Skald** runtime.

</Aside>

## The problem

A real AI system is a pile of loosely related parts: a dataset somewhere, a model
artifact in object storage, a prompt pasted into a notebook, an agent wired up in
application code, an eval that ran once. Each lives in a different tool with a
different shape, and nothing ties them together. When the model changes, the eval
that should have re-run doesn't, the prompt drifts, and no record connects the
three.

Wyrd makes each of those parts a **Card** — a typed, versioned declaration with a
stable shape — and keeps the connections between them explicit instead of implied.

## When to reach for Wyrd

- You want one versioned record for a dataset, model, or prompt instead of a
  filename convention and a wiki page.
- You are building agents or multi-step workflows and want them defined once and
  runnable from both Python and Rust.
- You want the relationships between components — this model was trained on that
  data, this agent uses that prompt — to be inspectable, not tribal knowledge.
- You want a registry that an **agent** can read and write through MCP, HTTP, or
  the CLI, not just a UI a human clicks.

If you only need to stash a single file, Wyrd is more than you need. It earns its
place when components have to stay connected over time.

## The model in one screen

Four ideas, in order:

1. **Card** — the durable, versioned envelope for one component. Every card shares
   the same outer shape: `apiVersion`, `metadata`, `kind`, `spec`,
   `relationships`, `status`.
2. **Spec** — the typed payload inside a card. `kind` selects the schema, so a
   `Data` card carries a `DataSpec` and a `Model` card carries a `ModelSpec`.
3. **Registry** — the server that stores cards, versions them, derives the
   relationship graph, and serves them over HTTP, MCP, and the CLI.
4. **Skald** — Wyrd's agent runtime. It runs the agents and workflows you declare,
   in Python or Rust, against the model providers you configure.

```yaml
apiVersion: wyrd/v1
metadata:
  space: default
  name: customer-churn
  version: "0.1.0"
  labels:
    domain: customer
kind: Data
spec:
  interface:
    kind: Pandas
relationships: []
status: null
```

One envelope across every kind means one inventory, one way to reference a
component (`CardRef`), one relationship graph, and one set of generated, agent-
readable contracts. A client never has to guess which shape a component uses
before it can read or validate it.

## What runs today

Wyrd is pre-release. Three card kinds are runnable end to end, the server and auth
stack are real, and the Skald runtime ships with full Python/Rust parity:

- **Cards** — author, save, load, and register `Data`, `Model`, and `Prompt`
  cards from Python.
- **Server & registry** — run `wyrd-server` against Postgres and object storage;
  upload and download cards over HTTP with API-key or OIDC-federated auth.
- **Skald** — define agents and workflows once and run them from Python or Rust
  against OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Vertex, or a mock provider.
- **Evaluation** — score agents with `run_eval` and the `wyrd eval` CLI.

The card *model* defines more kinds than are runnable yet. The
[Cards overview](/cards/) shows every kind with an honest status badge, and the
[Roadmap](/roadmap/) covers what is specified but not yet wired.

## Where execution lives

Wyrd records, versions, links, and serves card declarations. Skald runs agents and
workflows. Everything else — training jobs, your inference servers, your
notebooks, your containers — stays in your runtime. Wyrd is the control layer
beside those systems, not a replacement for them.

